<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yao Xing</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">366–416</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> · Also known as Emperor Wenhuan of Later Qin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yao Xing was the second ruler of the Later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a>, from 394 to 416. He defeated the remnants of the Former Qin and the Western Qin and ruled much of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>, where he brought the monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumarajiva">Kumarajiva</a> in 401 to direct the translation of Buddhist scriptures into Chinese.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Yao Xing, courtesy name Zilüe, was born in 366, when his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yao-chang">Yao Chang</a> was a general of the Former Qin emperor Fu Jiān. His mother is uncertain: the later Empress She is named, but he afterwards honoured his father's concubine Consort Sun as empress dowager. He served as an assistant to Fu Jiān's crown prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fu-hong">Fu Hong</a>, and when Yao Chang rebelled in 384 he fled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> to join him. Made crown prince in 386, he often guarded the home base and was known for his study of literature.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Soon after his accession <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fu-deng">Fu Deng</a> attacked, but his army, short of water near Mawei, was routed; Yao Xing then ambushed, captured and executed him, and Later Qin took nearly all of Former Qin's remaining lands. Historians describe these years as diligent and efficient rule. His brother Yao Chóng captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> from Jin in 399, and the same year, after astrological signs of disaster, Yao Xing gave up the imperial title for that of Heavenly King.</p><p>In 400 he defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qifu-gangui">Qifu Gangui</a> of Western Qin and took most of his cities, and in 401 the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuwei">Guzang</a> brought the submission of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/later-liang">Later Liang</a>, while Southern Liang, Northern Liang and Western Liang declared themselves <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>. His campaign against Northern Wei ended in 402 when Yao Ping's army was captured at Chaibi. Under the influence of Kumārajīva he became a devout Buddhist; the monk translated more than 300 sutras at his request, and he built many towers and temples.</p><p>Later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> then stopped expanding. In 407 the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helian-bobo">Liu Bobo</a>, later Helian Bobo, rebelled and founded Xia, wearing down Later Qin with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> attacks, and in 408 Qi Nan's whole army was captured, costing all of modern northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a>. Qifu Gangui restored Western Qin in 409, and Jin destroyed Southern Yan in 410 and Western Shu in 413, both of them Later Qin vassals.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His last years were troubled by his son Yao Bi, who plotted against the crown prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yao-hong">Yao Hong</a> and was twice forgiven. In 416, as Yao Xing returned ill from Huayin, Yao Yin and the attendant Yin Chong attacked the palace; Yao Xing appeared in person, his son's troops deserted, and he ordered Yao Bi to commit suicide. He died the next day. Yao Hong faced revolts by his brothers and attacks by Xia and Jin, and by 417 Later Qin had fallen to Jin.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yao Xing” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Yao Chang's son <b>Yao Xing</b> defeated Former Qin remnants and Western Qin, made Later Liang submit and ruled much of north-western China.</blockquote><a href="/state/later-qin">Later Qin</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Former Qin. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xian">Xi'an</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.26,108.94&amp;year=398" aria-label="Xi'an on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Yao Xing"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4223 4598h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4223 4598h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=34.26,108.94&amp;year=398">What was here in 398 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
